ed criteria for B and D. We could bring up Candidate A or C to ensure coverage. According to the process, we should discuss under-discussed candidates before final recommendation. So we should now introduce Candidate A or C. Perhaps bring up Candidate A's ability to anticipate dangerous situations and leadership, but also concerns about criticism and organization. Compare with D or B. This will add new discussion.\n\nWe need to reference prior discussion: we have been debating B vs D. Now we can say: \"While we have focused on B and D, we still need to evaluate A and C.\" Provide evidence: A anticipates dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership, but not good at taking criticism and can be unorganized. Explain implications: Anticipation of danger is crucial for long-haul safety, but lack of openness to criticism could affect feedback orientation. Compare with D's responsibility and problem solving. Suggest that A might be a strong alternative.\n\nThus we can shift our recommendation? We could keep D but note that A might be considered. But we need to state our current recommendation. We could keep D, but note that we need to discuss A and C before final. So we can say we still recommend D, but we need to evaluate A and C.\n\nThus public message: I agree with the need to compare B and D, but we haven't yet examined A and C. I propose we now discuss Candidate A, whose ability to anticipate dangerous situations and strong leadership could be valuable, though his difficulty taking criticism and disorganization are concerns. This adds a new perspective. My provisional vote remains D, pending discussion of A and C.\n\nMetadata: vote D.\n\nProceed."}
